          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My cholesterol came back at 6.4.",
          "answer": "A total cholesterol of 6.4 is a number a clinician will want to interpret, but the sources I searched don't give reference ranges for total cholesterol, so I can't tell you where 6.4 sits on them.\n\n**What the test measures:** A lipid panel usually includes total cholesterol, LDL cholesterol, HDL cholesterol and triglycerides. The lipid guidance I found says a non-fasting sample is acceptable for initial screening in most adults, and a fasting sample is preferred when triglycerides are markedly raised (*Lipids: screening intervals, risk stratification and statin initiation*). It also says the decision about treatment is driven by estimated 10-year cardiovascular risk, not by one number on its own. So total cholesterol is best read alongside the other parts of the panel and your overall risk profile.\n\n**Units:** The sources don't cover mmol/L against mg/dL conversion. Check the units printed on your report, because the same kind of number in the other unit would mean something quite different.\n\n**Lifestyle and follow-up:** The lifestyle notes I found cover blood pressure, diabetes, heart failure and reflux, not cholesterol specifically, so I can't describe cholesterol-focused diet or activity guidance from these sources. A clinician would typically look at the full panel, including LDL, HDL and triglycerides, together with your age, blood pressure, smoking status, diabetes status and family history, before suggesting any next steps.\n\n**Suggested next step:** Ask the clinician who ordered the test to review the full lipid panel with you and explain what the 6.4 means for you specifically. If you have a copy of the report, bring it, including the units and whether the sample was fasting.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
